Let venus back its memory with a host pointer the driver imports.

venus gives the guest host-visible memory by allocating it in the host's Vulkan
driver and sharing it out as a dma-buf or an opaque fd. A driver that exports
neither cannot be used this way at all, and Metal has neither to give:
MoltenVK's memory lives in MTLBuffers, not in file descriptors. This is the same
wall the WSL investigation hit through dzn, and it is where a Mac stops with
RESOURCE_CREATE_BLOB failing and the guest reporting itself out of host memory.

The way past it is to run the sharing the other way round. MoltenVK supports
VK_EXT_external_memory_host — vkAllocateMemory importing a pointer the caller
already owns, which underneath is Metal's newBufferWithBytesNoCopy. So the
renderer allocates the memory itself, as shared memory it holds a descriptor
for, hands the pointer to the driver, and hands the same descriptor to the guest
as a VIRGL_RESOURCE_FD_SHM blob. The GPU and the guest then address one set of
pages rather than two with a copy between them, and on a machine with unified
memory there is nothing further to pay.

Three parts, and only the first is Darwin-shaped:

- the physical device learns whether the driver imports host pointers, and what
  alignment it asks of one. Consulted only when the driver exports nothing, so
  a host that can export is unaffected;
- the device enables VK_EXT_external_memory_host when it will be used, and
  vkAllocateMemory takes the new path ahead of the fd paths for host-visible
  memory. vkr_device_memory remembers the descriptor, the pointer and the size;
- exporting a blob from such memory hands back the descriptor it was made from,
  because there is nothing to export: the fd already describes the pages.

Separately: the renderer now asks the loader for portability enumeration when
the loader offers it. A portability driver is hidden from an instance that has
not said it accepts one, so without this the host's vkCreateInstance answers
VK_ERROR_INCOMPATIBLE_DRIVER — which travels back through venus and reaches the
guest as a Vulkan with no drivers at all. Asked for only when present, so hosts
with ordinary drivers are unaffected.

Verified on an Apple M1: a Vulkan program in the guest clears an image on the
GPU and reads the pixels back, through venus, through MoltenVK, on Metal.

